00:05:04.758 --> 00:05:07.270
who come on the show to tell
a little bit about themselves,

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:08.944
what your background
is, what do you do.

00:05:08.944 --> 00:05:09.610
JEFF DEAN: Sure.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:12.829
So at the moment, I am
leading Google's AI division.

00:05:12.829 --> 00:05:15.120
Internally, we know that as
Google Research and Machine

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.164
Intelligence, but externally,
we can use Google AI sometimes,

00:05:18.164 --> 00:05:19.580
depending on who
we're talking to.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:23.720
And so that organization
does lots of different kinds

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:24.980
of computer science research.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.930
We do fundamental
research, trying

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:30.680
to create new logarithms and new
techniques to solve problems.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.950
We do a fair amount of work
with Google product areas

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.680
to work with interesting
research problems

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.290
in the context of products
and get sort of our research

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:41.930
out into the Google products
that everyone knows and loves

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.620
and uses.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.560
We also have been doing a bit
more work on sort of systems

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.140
infrastructure for
enabling our research,

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:55.040
but then also creating tools
that enable internal developers

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.735
at Google but also
external developers

00:05:56.735 --> 00:05:57.860
when we open source things.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.740
So TensorFlow, for example,
came out of our group

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.380
and is a popular machine
learning toolkit.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.500
We feel like that's a
good way to really broaden

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.630
the impact of the
research that we're doing,

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:12.290
is to make it easy for people
to then build on the work

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.845
that we're doing and have
good tools available.

00:06:14.845 --> 00:06:16.220
And then the fourth
kind of thing

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.970
that we generally do is try
to find new areas that Google

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.270
doesn't currently work in where
we think our research can make

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:23.910
a big impact.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.076
So we've been doing a lot
of work on sort of machine

00:06:26.076 --> 00:06:28.790
learning for health care,
machine learning for robotics,

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.670
because we think those are
kind of new emerging areas

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:33.800
where machine learning
and our research

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.150
will make a big difference.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.210
MELANIE WARRICK: In terms of the
work that your team is doing,

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:39.380
what does it mean for you?

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:40.670
And what does it
look like for you

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.003
now that-- because I know
this is a fairly new position,

00:06:43.003 --> 00:06:45.470
to a degree, that you're in
as the leader of Google AI

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:46.370
this year, right?

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:47.203
JEFF DEAN: Yes, yes.

00:06:47.203 --> 00:06:50.730
I took on this role in, I
guess, late March, early April.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:54.980
And basically my goal is to
inspire our great researchers

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.985
to do great research
and to tackle

00:06:56.985 --> 00:06:58.610
the important ambitious
problems that I

00:06:58.610 --> 00:06:59.870
think we should be working on.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.100
I spend Mondays coding.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.480
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, wow.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:03.938
JEFF DEAN: With my
colleague Sanjay

00:07:03.938 --> 00:07:06.350
Ghemawat, who I've been
working with for many years.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.590
I kind of probably have
four or five other projects

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.206
that I'm involved in, in
an actual technical level,

00:07:12.206 --> 00:07:13.580
where I go to the
weekly meetings

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.250
and make sort of technical
suggestions and so on.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:18.260
And then the rest
of my time is spent

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.530
kind of trying to steer a
reasonably large organization

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:23.596
to do the right things
and to make sure

00:07:23.596 --> 00:07:25.970
that we're focused on the
right problems and have impact.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:26.150
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:27.680
MELANIE WARRICK: I'm
curious, what do you code in?

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:28.760
JEFF DEAN: Mostly C++.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:29.816
MARK MANDEL: Oh, fun.

00:07:29.816 --> 00:07:31.190
So, I like asking
these questions

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:32.731
for the people who
work in AI and ML,

00:07:32.731 --> 00:07:34.520
because we get varying answers.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.120
What does AI mean to you?

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.150
JEFF DEAN: It means
basically being

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:41.960
able to have computer systems
that can solve problems

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:44.294
that you would think of as
requiring human intelligence.

00:07:44.294 --> 00:07:45.793
MARK MANDEL: I think
that's actually

00:07:45.793 --> 00:07:47.490
the most concise
answer we've ever got.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:48.710
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah,
pretty straightforward.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:50.840
Well, in terms of the work
that you do, what do you

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:53.173
think are some of the biggest
challenges that you are up

00:07:53.173 --> 00:07:53.854
against?

00:07:53.854 --> 00:07:55.520
JEFF DEAN: Really,
it's an exciting time

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.620
to be in the field of
computer science research

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.490
and machine learning
research, because there

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.180
is an explosion of
interest in this field, as

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:05.347
in the last maybe
seven or eight years,

00:08:05.347 --> 00:08:06.930
collectively, as a
research community,

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:10.010
we've really pushed forward
what computers can do.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:13.730
I think this is sort of most
striking in the computer vision

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:16.760
field where essentially
using deep learning

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.730
models for computer vision has
suddenly transformed computers

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.116
from not being able to
see very well to now

00:08:22.116 --> 00:08:23.240
actually being able to see.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.220
And if you think about
the impact on the world

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:26.780
that that will
have, and is already

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.790
having, that's transformative.

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:31.460
Suddenly you can have
automated machines

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:33.179
able to perceive the
world around them,

00:08:33.179 --> 00:08:36.289
able to sort of understand
what they're looking at.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:40.830
And that has huge implications
for general computer vision.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:44.510
So, it's a key enabler for
a lot of the experience

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:47.029
that Google Photos
provides to Google users.

00:08:47.029 --> 00:08:49.070
We can understand that's
a picture of a doberman,

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.570
and that's a mountain.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.360
But it also means transformative
things in health care.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.630
So tons and tons of medical
imaging-related problems

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:00.380
now are sort of tackle-able
with automated machine

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.000
learning algorithms
and assisting

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.470
sort of medical
professionals in that way.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:03.800
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.370
JEFF DEAN: Robotics,
it's very clear.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.030
If you want to build a
robot, it's very helpful

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:08.955
if the robot can see.

00:09:08.955 --> 00:09:09.830
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:11.586
JEFF DEAN: So, that
has big implications.

00:09:11.586 --> 00:09:13.460
And I think we're making
significant progress

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:15.567
in other fields related
to machine learning,

00:09:15.567 --> 00:09:17.900
so things like natural language
understanding and speech

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:18.720
recognition.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.560
You've seen tremendous advances
over the last six or seven

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:22.650
years.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:27.770
And that really means there's
all these opportunities

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:31.100
about how we should be
using these new capabilities

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.350
to affect the world, to
make it a better place

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:36.900
to build new things that
we couldn't build before.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:38.570
And I think that's
the excitement.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:41.790
And the flood of interest
in studying this field, how

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:43.290
do people do research
in this field,

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:46.760
how can more people enter
this field and do research?

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.770
MELANIE WARRICK: So we
spoke earlier this year

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.410
with Dr. Fei-Fei
Li around Cloud AI.

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:54.924
We spoke not too long ago with
Raia Hadsell out of DeepMind,

00:09:54.924 --> 00:09:56.840
and talked about some
of the robotics research

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:57.890
that they're doing.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:02.240
How does Brain and
Google AI collaborate

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:03.324
with the different groups?

00:10:03.324 --> 00:10:04.031
JEFF DEAN: Right.

00:10:04.031 --> 00:10:06.140
So, we actually have
strong collaborations

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.570
across all of Google
and even across all

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:10.500
of Alphabet in many areas.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.590
So with Cloud AI,
for example, many

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:15.500
of the sort of things that
they are bringing to market

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.520
started as research projects
in the Google AI Research

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:20.020
Division.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:21.850
And we're now
collaborating closely

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:25.450
to bring things like
the AutoML research

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.630
that was started into
products like Cloud AutoML,

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:32.350
where now customers who have
Computer Vision or other kinds

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:35.410
of problems that
maybe don't have

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.810
sophisticated machine
learning developers on staff

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:40.060
but want to be able
to take advantage

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:44.290
of the machine learning
capabilities that now exist, we

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.750
have systems that can
automatically train and learn

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.014
to solve new problems that
a customer might have.

00:10:49.014 --> 00:10:51.430
They might have pictures of
broken parts on their assembly

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:52.930
line and not broken
parts and they

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:54.460
want to be able to distinguish.

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:57.820
And they can essentially
just upload those images

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:59.620
and get a trained
model that helps them

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:02.890
with that particular
task, even without having,

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:04.960
sort of, a master's
level machine

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.242
learning expert on staff.

00:11:07.242 --> 00:11:08.450
MELANIE WARRICK: Makes sense.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.070
I know one of your areas
of passion in particular,

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:12.820
and where you've had
a significant impact,

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:17.080
especially, is around the Tensor
Processing Units, the TPUs.

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:20.020
What helped drive or inspire
the initial development

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:20.520
of the TPUs?

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.478
And I know you've had
this conversation before,

00:11:22.478 --> 00:11:25.210
but, you know, especially
from your experience

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:26.920
as you were coming
along and getting

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.930
into technology
to begin with, how

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.102
did that come about for you?

00:11:31.102 --> 00:11:31.810
JEFF DEAN: Right.

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:36.430
So I've always been interested
in how can computers

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:37.690
solve interesting problems.

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:40.330
And one interesting
way of doing that is

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:45.010
to bring more computation to
bear on problems that you can

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.080
then use more
sophisticated algorithms

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:51.850
or you can use larger data
sets to derive better insights.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:54.370
And so having more
computation is generally

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:56.170
a good thing in computing.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:12:00.940
And around the time
of maybe 2011, 2012,

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:03.761
when the Google Brain
Project that I co-founded

00:12:03.761 --> 00:12:05.260
was just getting
started, we started

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.200
to collaborate with a number
of different product teams

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.840
at Google to use deep learning
in some of the products.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:13.010
And the ones we collaborated
with most closely with

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:15.640
were the Speech
Recognition team to replace

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.659
kind of the older style
machine learning model

00:12:17.659 --> 00:12:19.450
that they were using
for speech recognition

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:22.661
with a deep learning-based model
for the acoustic portion of it.

00:12:22.661 --> 00:12:24.160
You know, the part
of the model that

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:28.180
goes from a very small audio
recording to part of a word.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:30.354
Is it "buh" or a
"fff" or a "sss"?

00:12:30.354 --> 00:12:31.270
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:32.410
JEFF DEAN: And then
there was another model

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:34.390
that we didn't
focus on initially,

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:36.550
kind of after that
stage of the processing.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:39.820
But replacing the acoustic
model with a deep learning-based

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:43.900
acoustic model gave a lot of
gains in recognition accuracy.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.810
And so we could tell that as
speech recognition gets better,

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:48.820
people are going to
use it more and more.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:50.820
And so I started to do
some back-of-the-envelope

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.490
calculations of like, well, what
happens if people start talking

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:55.840
to their phone three
minutes a day, right?

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.298
Because they're going to draft
all their emails with speech

00:12:58.298 --> 00:12:58.850
or something.

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:01.800
And at the time, we had
just lots and lots of CPUs

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:02.720
in our data center.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:04.510
And if you looked at
how much computation

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:07.750
that would be required if
100 million of our users

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:09.760
started to do that,
that was actually

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:10.962
kind of daunting and scary.

00:13:10.962 --> 00:13:13.420
We would have to essentially
double the computing footprint

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:17.020
of Google just to support
like a slightly better speech

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:19.792
recognition model for a
modest fraction of our users.

00:13:19.792 --> 00:13:21.000
MELANIE WARRICK: Seems legit.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.480
JEFF DEAN: Yeah, so it
seems a little scary.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:22.960
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:24.960
JEFF DEAN: But it's clear
we want to deploy that

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.149
to our users, because the
gains in recognition accuracy

00:13:28.149 --> 00:13:29.440
are actually quite significant.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:31.840
And so we started
to look at well,

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:35.230
what could we do for these
kinds of deep learning models

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:37.390
that would be more
computationally efficient?

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.940
And there are two
really nice properties

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:41.570
that deep learning models have.

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:45.310
So first, they are very
tolerant of reduced precision.

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:47.470
So essentially, if
you do the precisions

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.190
to one decimal
digit of accuracy,

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:52.190
that's actually perfectly
fine for these models.

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:54.148
You don't need six or
seven digits of precision

00:13:54.148 --> 00:13:57.040
like you would in floating
point computations or even

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:59.110
more in double combinations.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:00.520
And the second
property they have

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.860
is that all the
algorithms are made up

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:06.850
of different compositions of
handful of building blocks,

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.280
essentially matrix
multiplies, vector dot

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.700
products linear
algebra-style operations.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.230
So if you can build
hardware that is only

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:18.220
designed to accelerate low
precision, linear algebra,

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:19.120
you're golden.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:21.820
And that enables you
to then really tailor

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:23.140
the hardware to do only that.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:26.680
It doesn't need to do twisty
branch-y C++ code of all kinds

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:28.810
of arbitrary things,
pointer dereferences,

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:30.670
just low-precision
linear algebra,

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:34.780
then suddenly you can rethink
how you would completely design

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:36.057
a computer to do only that.

00:14:36.057 --> 00:14:38.140
MARK MANDEL: Since it's
focused in exactly what it

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:41.200
is it can do, does that mean
there are certain applications

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:42.977
that are better
or worse for a TPU

00:14:42.977 --> 00:14:44.560
that you might use
something else for,

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:45.760
or how does that work?

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:46.468
JEFF DEAN: Right.

00:14:46.468 --> 00:14:48.900
So this is one of the
double-edged swords

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.250
of specialization is if
you over-specialize, then

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:52.720
it's not generally applicable.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:55.160
But if you make something
very generally applicable,

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:56.770
then it doesn't get all the
performance benefits you

00:14:56.770 --> 00:14:58.060
could get from specialization.

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:01.670
And we actually chose, I think,
a pretty good balance for TPUs.

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:03.940
They're essentially
designed to accelerate

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:06.640
exactly the kinds of operations
you find in deep learning

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:07.240
models.

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:10.690
So, they're not general-purpose
sort of computational devices.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.330
But they are not tailored
to a particular model.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:15.400
They are tailored
to all the kinds

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:17.940
of operations you generally
find deep learning models.

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:19.830
And so you can run
vision models on them.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:20.950
You can run speech models.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:22.830
You can run recurrent
language models.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:26.419
The first TPU, TPU
v1, was targeted

00:15:26.419 --> 00:15:27.960
at only inference,
where you actually

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.746
need even less precision
than the training process.

00:15:30.746 --> 00:15:32.370
And that was our most
pressing problem.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:34.786
That was how we could get the
speech recognition model out

00:15:34.786 --> 00:15:38.070
to the world without doubling
Google's data center footprint.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:42.420
And then the second
TPU was really

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:44.670
targeted at both
training and inference.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.650
And the reason we didn't
bite that off first

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:49.811
was that training is a much
more complex hardware design

00:15:49.811 --> 00:15:50.310
problem.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.920
Because for
inference, usually you

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:54.750
can design things so
that a single model

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:55.906
will fit on one chip.

00:15:55.906 --> 00:15:58.280
And if you need more capacity,
you just kind of stamp out

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.127
lots of copies of
that system and put

00:16:00.127 --> 00:16:01.710
a lot of boards in
a lot of computers,

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:04.200
and all of a sudden you have
lots of speech recognition

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.340
capacity.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:09.280
For training, it's very
unlikely for large models

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:11.130
you can get a single
chip to be fast enough

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.980
to train a model very fast.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:15.720
And what you really
want in training

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:19.350
is fast turnaround for
machine learning experiments

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:22.980
or huge scalability so you can
train very large models on very

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:25.200
large data sets
very quickly and be

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:28.050
able to iterate from a machine
learning research perspective.

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:29.567
Or a deployed
system, you know, you

00:16:29.567 --> 00:16:31.650
might want to retrain your
model every 10 minutes,

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:33.320
because your data changes.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:36.007
There, it's not just
a chip design problem.

00:16:36.007 --> 00:16:37.590
It's actually a whole
computer system.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:39.486
It's more like a
supercomputer design problem

00:16:39.486 --> 00:16:40.860
where you have
the chips and then

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:42.510
you have some
complicated interconnect

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:43.676
that connects them together.

00:16:43.676 --> 00:16:47.280
You have cooling issues for how
do you cool this giant thing?

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:48.960
It's made up of multiple racks.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:52.530
And so that's why we didn't bite
that off in the first system,

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:55.530
but chose to wait till the
second iteration of the TPU

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.531
design to tackle
training as well.

00:16:57.531 --> 00:16:59.780
MELANIE WARRICK: And you
announced this year version 3

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:00.875
of TPUs as well.

00:17:00.875 --> 00:17:01.500
JEFF DEAN: Yes.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.416
MELANIE WARRICK: And am
I right in remembering

00:17:03.416 --> 00:17:05.214
that the cooling
system, it's now liquid?

00:17:05.214 --> 00:17:05.880
JEFF DEAN: Yeah.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:06.119
MELANIE WARRICK: Fancy.

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:07.589
JEFF DEAN: So it turns out--
yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:08.550
it is fancy.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:11.220
Any time you mix computers and
water, it's always exciting.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:16.720
So the TPU v3, which Sundar
announced at Google I/O

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:20.660
in May of 2018, essentially
got water cooling.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:23.280
So a board has four
of these chips on it,

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:24.810
and the water
cooling kind of goes

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:27.810
to the surface of these chips
and takes excess heat away.

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.600
And the largest
footprint deployment--

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:32.300
we call these things pods--

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:35.190
the TPU v2 pods were
essentially made up

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:40.200
of 64 of those devices,
256 TPU v2 chips.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:43.720
And the pods scale for
the TPU v3 is much larger.

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:46.260
So we have about eight
times as much computation

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:48.692
in the TPU v3 pod as we
did in the TPU v2 pod.

00:17:48.692 --> 00:17:51.150
MELANIE WARRICK: I know a couple
of the people on your team

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:54.090
was specifically running a
Stanford DAWNBench on it,

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:55.710
and they were showing
the differences

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:57.184
in terms of its performance.

00:17:57.184 --> 00:17:59.850
Right now all that's going in my
head right now as the ImageNet,

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:00.180
but--

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:00.590
JEFF DEAN: I call do that.

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:01.225
MELANIE WARRICK:
--DAWNBench-- please.

00:18:01.225 --> 00:18:04.080
JEFF DEAN: So, DAWNBench
is a new machine learning

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.350
benchmark set up by a
research group at Stanford,

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:09.330
the DAWN research
group, that is designed

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:11.520
to look at a variety of
different kinds of machine

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.660
learning problems, both training
and inference, and measures

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:16.910
a few different metrics.

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:20.070
So one of them is for a
particular machine learning

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:24.090
problem, how quickly can you get
to a certain level of accuracy

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:26.980
that is sort of a good level
of accuracy for that problem?

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:30.420
And one of them is a ImageNet
processing benchmark,

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.470
where you have to get
to 76% top-1 accuracy.

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.470
MELANIE WARRICK: And this
is classifying images.

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:34.870
JEFF DEAN: Classifying
images, right.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.600
MELANIE WARRICK: ImageNet
is very much like that.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:37.080
JEFF DEAN: Right, yeah.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:37.830
MELANIE WARRICK: And
this is what you--

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:39.372
JEFF DEAN: You
get a color image.

00:18:39.372 --> 00:18:41.580
You have to classify it into
one of 1,000 categories.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:43.970
Its actually pretty
hard, human error rate

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:47.403
in the top-1
accuracy is about 5%.

00:18:47.403 --> 00:18:48.597
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:18:48.597 --> 00:18:50.680
JEFF DEAN: Because its got
like 40 breeds of dogs,

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:51.820
and you should be
able to distinguish--

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:54.153
MARK MANDEL: And so I'm
guessing the TPUs did pretty OK?

00:18:54.153 --> 00:18:56.370
JEFF DEAN: Yeah, so the
TPUs took the top three

00:18:56.370 --> 00:19:00.330
spots in the overall
time metric with pods.

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:04.260
And then also the top two
spots, I believe, in the cost

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:07.020
using public cloud
computing resources.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:07.950
MELANIE WARRICK: Cool.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.670
Well, what do you enjoy most
about working with these chips

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.840
and working with the
parallel processing?

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:15.750
JEFF DEAN: I actually
have been excited

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:17.820
about parallel processing
for quite some time,

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:21.690
ever since I took a course
my senior year at University

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:23.879
of Minnesota on
parallel processing.

00:19:23.879 --> 00:19:25.920
And at the time, I was
really excited about that,

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.290
because I felt like more
computation was the answer

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:31.050
to solving a lot more problems.

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:34.540
And this was kind of during
the first wave of excitement

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:36.540
about neural nets that
happened in the late '80s

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.690
and early '90s, when new
algorithms were developed

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:42.060
and when it was first
surfacing as a potentially

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:44.820
interesting way to tackle
some difficult problems.

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:47.100
And at that time,
basically neural nets

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.950
could get interesting results
on very toy-sized problems,

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:53.189
but not sort of scale to
really interesting problems.

00:19:53.189 --> 00:19:55.230
And so there was a wave
of excitement about this.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:56.730
But then in this
wave of excitement,

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:58.710
I kind of felt like
more computation

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:00.660
was going to be
helpful for letting

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:01.850
us tackle bigger problems.

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.670
And so I did an undergrad thesis
working with the professor who

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:07.230
taught the parallel and
distributed computing class

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.720
I took, sort of did
an independent thesis

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.400
on parallel training
of neural nets.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.130
And so I felt like if we
could take the 64 processor

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:16.142
machine we had in the
department and, you know,

00:20:16.142 --> 00:20:18.350
apply parallel methods to
it, we could get, you know,

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:20.810
a factor of 60 more compute
applied to these things,

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:24.230
and then we could scale up
and tackle bigger problems.

00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:27.350
In retrospect, we needed a
million times more computation,

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:28.520
not 60.

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:32.540
And so if you essentially just
wait it out with Moore's Law--

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:35.450
at least until about 5 or 10
years ago, when Moore's Law

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:36.600
significantly slowed down.

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:40.130
But before that, you know, the
previous 25 years, 40 years,

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:43.850
even, we were getting these
consistent 2x performance

00:20:43.850 --> 00:20:46.160
improvement every two years.

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:47.600
You just wait long
enough, and all

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:49.766
of a sudden, voila, a million
times as much compute.

00:20:49.766 --> 00:20:50.870
And you're golden.

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:55.180
And that's sort of what started
to happen in 2008, 2009.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:57.740
It was we suddenly had
enough compute, initially

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:00.440
using GPU cards, which
were designed for gaming,

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:03.050
but obviously have turned out
to be quite useful for training

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:05.960
deep learning models, because
a lot of the acceleration you

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:08.450
want to do for
graphics processing

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:12.410
turns out to be amenable to
training deep learning models.

00:21:12.410 --> 00:21:16.070
And so that was when people
in the academic community

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:19.190
started to really see
that we were starting

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:21.921
to get interesting results
on real problems using

00:21:21.921 --> 00:21:22.420
neural nets.

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:22.850
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:24.320
JEFF DEAN: So there
was kind of excitement

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:25.500
started to build back up.

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:27.333
MELANIE WARRICK: What
was one of the hardest

00:21:27.333 --> 00:21:28.490
parts in your career?

00:21:28.490 --> 00:21:31.250
It wasn't like AI
was well-accepted

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:35.390
for many, many years when you
were studying between then

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:37.220
and 2006 really, right?

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.470
So what was some of
the hardest moments

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:42.170
in terms of pursuing
this interest,

00:21:42.170 --> 00:21:43.364
pursuing this research?

00:21:43.364 --> 00:21:44.030
JEFF DEAN: Yeah.

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:49.430
I actually kind of come at this
as a general computer systems

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:49.930
person.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.910
So I like to view
the kind of lens

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:55.880
I bring to problems is,
there's an interesting problem.

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:58.100
Let's figure out how we
can build a computer system

00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:01.370
to tackle that problem,
often involving, you know,

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:05.150
either kind of good abstractions
for expressing computations

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:08.742
we want to perform or using
large amounts of computation

00:22:08.742 --> 00:22:10.700
and figure out how to
parallelize or distribute

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:12.320
computation across
lots of machines

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:15.710
so that we can sort of
scale up the competition.

00:22:15.710 --> 00:22:19.190
So, although I was doing
work as an undergrad

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:21.020
on this parallel
training of neural nets

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:23.720
I was coming at it from
the perspective of, how can

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:25.880
we built a computer
system to do this?

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:28.670
And then I sort of
started to get back

00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:32.060
interested in machine
learning in 2011

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:34.037
when I happened to
bump into Andrew Ng

00:22:34.037 --> 00:22:35.120
a microkitchen at Google--

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:35.690
MELANIE WARRICK: It
was going to say,

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:36.980
the story goes you were
in a microkitchen, right?

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:37.813
JEFF DEAN: Yes, yes.

00:22:37.813 --> 00:22:39.650
So, I knew him a little
bit, and so I said,

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:40.370
oh, what are you doing here?

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:42.680
He's like, oh, I'm spending
a day a week at Google X,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.870
and I haven't quite figured
out what I'm doing here yet,

00:22:44.870 --> 00:22:46.453
but, you know, my
students at Stanford

00:22:46.453 --> 00:22:48.209
are starting to see
what kinds of problems

00:22:48.209 --> 00:22:50.000
can be solved with
neural nets, and they're

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:51.166
getting pretty good results.

00:22:51.166 --> 00:22:52.400
I'm like, oh, really?

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:53.210
That's pretty cool.

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:56.260
I did a bit of work on
neural nets as an undergrad.

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:57.920
I said, they're
really working now?

00:22:57.920 --> 00:22:58.957
And he's like, yeah.

00:22:58.957 --> 00:23:01.040
So I said, we should train
really big neural nets.

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:03.230
And so that was
sort of the genesis

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:07.280
of how we decided to form the
Google Brain Project along

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:08.030
with Greg Corrado.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:12.810
And we basically, at that
time, didn't have accelerators

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:13.650
or data centers.

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:15.316
But we said, we have
a lot of computers.

00:23:15.316 --> 00:23:17.610
Let's figure out how to make
a computer system that can

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:18.570
train really big neural nets--

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:19.100
MELANIE WARRICK:
Let's leverage this.

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:20.320
JEFF DEAN: --using CPUs.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:22.730
MARK MANDEL: And so is that
the moment where you like,

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:24.500
this is going to be
a really big thing.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:25.520
Do you think it then?

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:27.710
JEFF DEAN: I did
feel like, you know,

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:31.370
once we kind of did a little bit
more work, maybe another month

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:34.100
of dabbling, then
it seemed like, hey,

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:38.660
we can actually tackle problems
using neural nets that people

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:41.180
have felt like they're the right
abstraction for a long time

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:44.690
but maybe they would suddenly
start to work in real problems

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:46.610
if we could apply
lots of computation.

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:49.160
And so that's really been
what a lot of my focus

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:52.160
has been on in the last
six or seven years,

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:56.120
is how can we bring lots of
computation to the problems

00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:58.280
that you want to tackle
with machine learning,

00:23:58.280 --> 00:23:59.810
and in particular deep learning.

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:01.851
MELANIE WARRICK: So, we're
recording this the day

00:24:01.851 --> 00:24:04.430
after the Neural Information
Processing Systems Conference--

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:04.820
JEFF DEAN: NIPS.

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:05.700
MELANIE WARRICK: --sold out.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:06.533
JEFF DEAN: Yes, yes.

00:24:06.533 --> 00:24:08.902
MELANIE WARRICK: In 11
minutes and so many seconds.

00:24:08.902 --> 00:24:09.860
JEFF DEAN: 48, I think.

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:10.550
MELANIE WARRICK: 48 seconds.

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:11.580
JEFF DEAN: 11 minutes
and 48 seconds.

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:11.840
Yes.

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:13.380
MELANIE WARRICK: I'm
using this as an example.

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:15.546
There's a lot of excitement,
clearly, in this field.

00:24:15.546 --> 00:24:17.390
How do you feel about
all this excitement?

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:19.556
JEFF DEAN: You know, there
is a fair amount of hype.

00:24:19.556 --> 00:24:21.350
But on the other hand,
the hype, some of it

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:23.600
is actually justified,
because we're now actually

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:27.380
able to build systems that
can solve problems we couldn't

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:29.270
solve five or 10 years ago.

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:30.710
I mentioned computer vision.

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:33.020
Speech recognition has
advanced tremendously.

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:34.790
Translation has advanced.

00:24:34.790 --> 00:24:36.920
Many applications of
these sorts of things

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:40.640
have shown real improvements
or new capabilities

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:42.240
in what computers can do.

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:45.570
So I think that's part of why
people are excited and many,

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:47.250
many people want
to enter the field.

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:49.325
So you see enrollment in
machine learning classes

00:24:49.325 --> 00:24:50.960
at undergraduate
and graduate levels

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:52.280
just going through the roof.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:55.700
You see more and more people
attending the major machine

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:56.709
learning conferences.

00:24:56.709 --> 00:24:58.250
You see the number
of research papers

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:00.710
on archive actually growing
at a faster exponential rate

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:04.040
than Moore's Law over the
last seven or eight years.

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:05.300
It's quite remarkable.

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:08.870
And, you know, I think
it's important to temper

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:10.700
people's expectations
who think we're

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:15.840
going to have super-intelligent
things that can solve problems,

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:18.780
general problems that
humans can't solve,

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:20.850
in the next six months.

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:22.350
That's kind of the
one level of hype

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:25.800
that's clearly not justified.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:27.390
But it's also
important to recognize

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:30.240
that computers can now see
that's a pretty big deal.

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:32.730
And that has major
implications across a bunch

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:33.540
of different areas.

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:35.250
You know, Google
here in Alphabet

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:36.570
are working on autonomous cars.

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:38.820
Clearly computer
vision and perception

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:41.970
is an important component of
making safe autonomous cars.

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:45.000
Robotics, machine learning
for medical imaging,

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.580
machine learning for other
medical applications,

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:49.560
health care
applications, these are

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:53.610
going to transform major
problems in the world.

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.170
And that's good.

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:56.370
That's why there's hype.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:58.860
It's important to tamp
down excessive hype,

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:01.260
but not to say that there
shouldn't be any hype,

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:04.230
because I think we are
making significant advances

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:05.880
in what computers can do.

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:07.796
MELANIE WARRICK: Any
application in particular

00:26:07.796 --> 00:26:10.980
that you've seen that you feel
don't get a lot of attention

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:12.264
but are pretty impactful?

00:26:12.264 --> 00:26:12.930
JEFF DEAN: Yeah.

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:17.690
I mean, I think there's a
realization that I've come to

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:20.160
from looking at a few
different problems researchers

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:23.220
in our group have tackled and
other work that's going on,

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:25.620
where in many
scientific problems,

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:28.530
there are for example,
traditional high-performance

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:31.080
computing-based simulations
of some property,

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.280
some scientific thing.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:34.640
Maybe you're simulating
chemical interactions

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:36.330
at a very fine grained level.

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:38.930
How do the electrons flow
around and what properties

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:39.960
does that have?

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:42.510
Or maybe you're simulating
earthquake faults

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:45.090
and you have a
very detailed sort

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:47.670
of physics-based model of how
slippage in earthquake faults

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:48.670
happens.

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:50.382
And often these
computational models

00:26:50.382 --> 00:26:52.090
are very, very expensive
computationally.

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:54.420
So, you know, you
run one chemical

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:55.860
through your
simulator and an hour

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:59.010
later, you get information
about the quantum

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:02.400
properties of this chemical or
what binds to what or things

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:03.180
like that.

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:06.030
And you can actually use
that simulator as a teacher.

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:10.200
And so if you have a really
expensive scientific simulator,

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:13.230
often you can train a neural net
to do what that simulator does.

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:14.982
Completely end-to-end,
you take the inputs

00:27:14.982 --> 00:27:16.440
you would have fed
to the simulator

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:19.260
and you get the outputs, and
you run a bunch of examples

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:20.340
through the simulator.

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:22.230
Now you have a training
set for a neural net.

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:24.750
And in a few different
domains, we've

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:26.400
actually seen people do this.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:30.450
And in, for example, the quantum
chemistry domain, George Dahl

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:33.840
and others in our research
group worked with some chemists

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:36.540
at different universities
and took the simulator

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:38.790
they were using, trained
a neural net to do it,

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:41.700
and now they can't
distinguish the accuracy

00:27:41.700 --> 00:27:44.550
versus the original sort
of HPC-style simulator.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:46.122
But the tool is
300,000 times faster.

00:27:46.122 --> 00:27:46.830
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:27:46.830 --> 00:27:49.339
JEFF DEAN: And any
time you take your tool

00:27:49.339 --> 00:27:50.880
and you make it
300,000 times faster,

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:53.670
that just transforms how
you would do science, right?

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:56.040
You can imagine, oh, I'm
going to go make coffee.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:58.012
Let me screen 100 million
molecules and then

00:27:58.012 --> 00:27:59.720
when I come back, I'll
look at the 10,000

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:00.840
that are most interesting.

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:04.020
And that same thing happened
with Brendan Meade, who

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:05.790
was a visiting Harvard
faculty member who

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:08.359
visited our group in
Cambridge for a year

00:28:08.359 --> 00:28:10.650
and did a bunch of work on
earthquake fault simulation.

00:28:10.650 --> 00:28:12.460
He relayed that
in work he'd just

00:28:12.460 --> 00:28:14.730
done before he came
to visit us, that he'd

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:19.015
replaced the earthquake fault
simulation inner loop with kind

00:28:19.015 --> 00:28:20.390
of the world's
lamest neural net.

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:24.120
It was, you know, four
layers of 10 neurons each.

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:26.820
And all of a sudden, the thing
was between 10,000 and 100,000

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:27.450
times faster.

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:27.690
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, wow.

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:28.730
JEFF DEAN: And he couldn't tell
the difference in accuracy.

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:29.410
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:31.618
JEFF DEAN: So, I think that's
a good lens to look at,

00:28:31.618 --> 00:28:33.540
you know, are you doing
scientific computing

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:35.190
or simulation of some sort?

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:38.910
Can you then train a neural net
to do an approximation of that

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:41.340
that is quite likely to
be accurate if you give it

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:42.360
enough training data?

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:43.380
MARK MANDEL: And now
I'm just thinking of--

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:44.090
MELANIE WARRICK:
Giving you ideas.

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:45.230
MARK MANDEL: Well, I
work with my games.

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:45.360
MELANIE WARRICK: I know, games.

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:46.110
MARK MANDEL: And
so, now I'm like,

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:47.280
if you can get that
kind of performance

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:48.300
for that level of
simulation, you

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:49.880
can do some really
interesting things.

00:28:49.880 --> 00:28:50.700
JEFF DEAN: Right, yeah.

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:52.380
MELANIE WARRICK: And I think I
saw you speak about this, too,

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:54.360
that you're using neural
nets as well to help

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.526
with improving the actual
performance of the systems

00:28:56.526 --> 00:28:57.630
themselves.

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.570
JEFF DEAN: There's a big
opportunity in general computer

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:01.200
systems.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:04.800
So if you think about what's at
the heart of operating systems

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:09.300
or compilers or storage systems
or other computer systems,

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:11.910
they're generally
laden with kind

00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:13.340
of hand-written heuristics.

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:15.600
You know, which process
should the operating system

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:18.600
schedule next, out of the ready
processes that are available?

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:21.330
Or for a compiler,
should it, you know,

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:24.420
emit code with this loop net
ordering or this loop ordering?

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:26.240
Or which, you know,
variable should it

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:29.130
spill the memory when it can't
fit them all in registers?

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:33.360
And in many cases, I
believe these are actually

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:34.200
learning problems.

00:29:34.200 --> 00:29:36.810
And so the heuristics that
people tend to handwrite

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:38.970
have to work well
in the general case.

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:41.640
And they can't really be
developed in such a way

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:45.280
that they completely adapt to
how the system is being used.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:49.920
So to give you an example, in
our Bigtable storage system,

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:52.740
you know, there's essentially
a key value store.

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:55.590
And client requests come
in to Bigtable servers,

00:29:55.590 --> 00:29:58.020
and they can request
that certain data be

00:29:58.020 --> 00:29:59.190
read from different tables.

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:02.810
And when that happens,
the Bigtable server

00:30:02.810 --> 00:30:04.560
decides to put it in
a named memory cache,

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:07.159
so if that data is needed
again, then, you know,

00:30:07.159 --> 00:30:08.200
you have it in the cache.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:09.950
You don't have to go
to disc and fetch it.

00:30:09.950 --> 00:30:12.541
And if you look,
lots of information

00:30:12.541 --> 00:30:14.790
is available at the time you
make the decision to read

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:16.030
the data from disc.

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:19.150
We know, for example,
the internal user group

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:21.720
of the job that's
running the process that

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:23.010
requested the data.

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:26.280
We know maybe the job
name that requested this.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:28.590
And you would never
write a heuristic--

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:32.490
so a lot of the processing is
from sequential MapReduce style

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:35.340
processing jobs that are going
to read the data once, never

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:37.410
use it again, until
an hour later when

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:38.661
a similar MapReduce starts up.

00:30:38.661 --> 00:30:40.910
But you don't really want
to insert that in the cache,

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:43.519
because you're not going to get
any reuse sort of immediately.

00:30:43.519 --> 00:30:45.060
But you would never
write a heuristic

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:46.601
in the middle of
your Bigtable server

00:30:46.601 --> 00:30:49.170
that says, if job name
starts with MapReduce dash,

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:51.550
then don't insert in the cache.

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:54.930
But if you look at what a
learning heuristic could do,

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:56.790
it would pretty quickly
learn the pattern

00:30:56.790 --> 00:31:00.000
that these jobs that seem
to start with MapReduce dash

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:02.280
never seem to reuse the
data I insert in the cache,

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:04.130
so I'm just not
going to insert it.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:06.840
And I think there's huge
potential for the thousands

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:09.870
and thousands and thousands of
heuristics in computer systems

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:14.190
to really mostly be learned and
adapt to how they're actually

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:14.691
being used.

00:31:14.691 --> 00:31:16.856
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
wonderful and exciting, too,

00:31:16.856 --> 00:31:19.540
to think about the ways that
this could be improved upon.

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:21.420
I feel like there's so many
