00:34:09.649 --> 00:34:11.940
The last couple things we
want to make sure we touch on

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:13.679
before we let you go.

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:16.350
Love to know what is one of
the best pieces of advice

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:18.600
that you've received
in your career.

00:34:18.600 --> 00:34:21.960
JEFF DEAN: I don't know if it's
a specific piece of advice that

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:24.270
I've received, but an
observation I've made

00:34:24.270 --> 00:34:27.960
throughout my career is
that really good way to do

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:31.350
interesting things is to partner
with people who know things

00:34:31.350 --> 00:34:32.219
that you don't.

00:34:32.219 --> 00:34:35.010
And I often find that working
on projects with three

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:37.199
or four people
where you each have

00:34:37.199 --> 00:34:39.081
kind of different
kinds of expertise

00:34:39.081 --> 00:34:40.830
and collectively you
kind of come together

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:44.159
to tackle some interesting
problem that none of you

00:34:44.159 --> 00:34:47.370
could solve individually,
but collectively you can,

00:34:47.370 --> 00:34:50.310
because you have the full
totality of techniques

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:51.540
or can develop them.

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:53.550
And the reason that's
really important

00:34:53.550 --> 00:34:57.170
is it's a good way to
continuously learn new things,

00:34:57.170 --> 00:34:58.545
even after you
get out of school.

00:34:58.545 --> 00:35:00.961
You know, I think when you're
in undergraduate or graduate

00:35:00.961 --> 00:35:03.690
school or whatever, you're
obviously learning things.

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:05.310
But it's important
to kind of continue

00:35:05.310 --> 00:35:08.100
to learn things and
pick up new ideas

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:09.810
and sort of
stimulate your brain.

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:11.642
And this is a really
good way to do that.

00:35:11.642 --> 00:35:13.725
And then you kind of go
off of that project and go

00:35:13.725 --> 00:35:16.058
onto a different project, and
some of the other people's

00:35:16.058 --> 00:35:19.390
expertise has kind of rubbed
off as a sheen on you.

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:21.820
And you can now at
least look at problems

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.100
through the lens of
the kinds of expertise

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:26.890
they have and say, oh, yeah,
that looks a lot like this.

00:35:26.890 --> 00:35:29.590
I know the questions to
ask or the right approach

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:32.064
to take in tackling
this, even if that's not

00:35:32.064 --> 00:35:33.355
your sort of formal background.

00:35:33.355 --> 00:35:33.640
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:34.556
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:35:34.556 --> 00:35:35.124
Definitely.

00:35:35.124 --> 00:35:37.540
You mentioned about Moore's
Law and the amount of research

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:39.800
that's coming out and
papers in particular.

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:42.820
How do you keep up
on the resources

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:44.434
and the research and all that?

00:35:44.434 --> 00:35:45.100
JEFF DEAN: Yeah.

00:35:45.100 --> 00:35:47.080
I mean, again, I think
this is something

00:35:47.080 --> 00:35:50.620
where surrounding yourself
with people who know things

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:54.550
that you don't is a really
good way, because if you have

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:57.130
a good network of people
that you interact with,

00:35:57.130 --> 00:35:59.440
they can partly be
your eyes and ears

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:02.650
and look out for interesting
things in their sort of area

00:36:02.650 --> 00:36:05.770
of focus and say, oh, yeah,
that's kind of interesting.

00:36:05.770 --> 00:36:07.750
Someone told me
about this today.

00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:10.150
And you can kind
of start to connect

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:14.710
the dots between different
areas and see the potential

00:36:14.710 --> 00:36:16.920
for a technique
developed over here,

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:19.420
how it might be used over here,
or how it might combine well

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:22.580
with some other technique and
really build on each other.

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:25.130
So I think it's very hard
to keep up with everything.

00:36:25.130 --> 00:36:27.250
So you need to sort
of collectively

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:31.360
find some things you really
do follow yourself, and then

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:34.370
get a cursory level of
information about other things.

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:37.330
One thing I've observed
is that a lot of people

00:36:37.330 --> 00:36:40.320
really, really deeply
read a research paper.

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:42.460
And I think that's
important sometimes,

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:45.820
but it's kind of the wrong
approach for general learning.

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:47.860
I think you'd be
much better served

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:51.370
to take the time to read
100 abstracts of 100

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:54.280
different papers and
sort of understand

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:56.410
the highest-level concept
that they each have.

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:59.230
Because you can always go
read that thing in more detail

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:00.820
if you need to find
out more about it.

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:03.232
But being able to have those
100 pieces of information of

00:37:03.232 --> 00:37:04.690
like, oh, yeah,
that's a technique.

00:37:04.690 --> 00:37:05.560
That's a thing.

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.322
We can kind of do
something like that.

00:37:07.322 --> 00:37:09.280
That's how you really
start to connect the dots

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:11.200
and make connections
across fields

00:37:11.200 --> 00:37:12.490
or across different problems.

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:13.240
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.115
MELANIE WARRICK: Any
specific public research

00:37:15.115 --> 00:37:17.509
you've heard of very
recently that specifically

00:37:17.509 --> 00:37:20.050
comes to your mind, that you're
like, this is exciting to me?

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:20.550
Or--

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:22.810
JEFF DEAN: Well, I
think the general idea

00:37:22.810 --> 00:37:26.200
of having computers
that can automatically

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:28.900
do experiments, and then
measure the results of those,

00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:30.870
and then sort of
continuously absorb

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:32.620
the results of a first
set of experiments,

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:34.540
and then generate
new experiments.

00:37:34.540 --> 00:37:37.270
So all of the neural
architectures search work

00:37:37.270 --> 00:37:39.712
or the AutoML work that
our group has been doing,

00:37:39.712 --> 00:37:41.170
a lot of the
reinforcement learning

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:44.170
based work that is showing
promise in solving games

00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:46.540
like Go or OpenAI work on Dota.

00:37:46.540 --> 00:37:48.995
I think all these
things kind of point

00:37:48.995 --> 00:37:51.290
at that general direction
of having computers

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:54.850
that can automatically explore
different approaches to solving

00:37:54.850 --> 00:37:57.010
problems is going
to be important.

00:37:57.010 --> 00:38:00.120
Because a computer can
run 50,000 experiments

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:03.400
whereas a human runs 50,
looks at the results,

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:05.822
and then figures out, what
are the next 50 to run?

00:38:05.822 --> 00:38:06.530
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:38:06.530 --> 00:38:06.940
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:08.356
And then the other
question I want

00:38:08.356 --> 00:38:10.960
to ask you, out of all the
interviews you've ever done,

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:13.300
what question do you wish
people would ask you?

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:15.974
JEFF DEAN: I will tell
