00:40:28.360 --> 00:40:29.270
Question of the week.

00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:31.070
MARK MANDEL: So we have our
wonderful friend here, Gabe.

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:31.778
GABE WEISS: Whee.

00:40:31.778 --> 00:40:32.550
MARK MANDEL: Hey.

00:40:32.550 --> 00:40:34.540
So Gabe, we have a
wonderful question for you,

00:40:34.540 --> 00:40:36.249
because we know you
do stuff in IoT Core.

00:40:36.249 --> 00:40:37.164
GABE WEISS: I'm ready.

00:40:37.164 --> 00:40:39.010
MARK MANDEL: If I have
an IoT Core thing,

00:40:39.010 --> 00:40:41.200
how do I get my
data from IoT Core

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:43.050
to display in real time
on a web front-end?

00:40:43.050 --> 00:40:44.800
I believe you've done
something like this.

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:45.550
GABE WEISS: I have, recently.

00:40:45.550 --> 00:40:47.304
So anyone that's
worked in IoT knows

00:40:47.304 --> 00:40:49.220
there's a bajillion
different ways to do this.

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:50.620
There's different
classes of devices.

00:40:50.620 --> 00:40:51.470
There different protocols.

00:40:51.470 --> 00:40:52.678
There's different everything.

00:40:52.678 --> 00:40:55.721
So there's-- how many grains
of sand on a beach are there?

00:40:55.721 --> 00:40:57.220
That's how many
ways are to do this.

00:40:57.220 --> 00:40:59.950
The way that we did it as
kind of our golden path

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:04.250
at Cloud Next in 2018, we
built an entire end-to-end app

00:41:04.250 --> 00:41:04.930
on stage.

00:41:04.930 --> 00:41:06.230
There are five of us.

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:07.240
So we went from devices.

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:10.000
We used a Raspberry
Pi with some sensors

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:12.010
that we did
measuring heart rate.

00:41:12.010 --> 00:41:14.270
And that will
communicate with MQTT,

00:41:14.270 --> 00:41:16.780
is the protocol that we used
that IoT Core understands

00:41:16.780 --> 00:41:17.470
how to receive.

00:41:17.470 --> 00:41:19.750
So you can send your telemetry
data from your devices

00:41:19.750 --> 00:41:20.750
to IoT Core.

00:41:20.750 --> 00:41:22.660
From IoT Core, it gets bridged.

00:41:22.660 --> 00:41:24.130
All of your telemetry
data becomes

00:41:24.130 --> 00:41:27.100
events in Cloud Pub/Sub, which
is our event stream manager.

00:41:27.100 --> 00:41:30.670
From Cloud Pub/Sub, you can
trigger Cloud Functions,

00:41:30.670 --> 00:41:33.100
which we did to pull the
telemetry data out of Pub/Sub

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:35.050
and put it into our
data warehouse, which

00:41:35.050 --> 00:41:36.310
was Cloud Firestore.

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:38.590
We picked Firestore because
it's a NoSQL database,

00:41:38.590 --> 00:41:40.970
it's super low latency,
and it has a special sauce,

00:41:40.970 --> 00:41:44.777
which is it has a push mechanism
so that front end apps can

00:41:44.777 --> 00:41:47.360
register for a callback when any
data changes in the database.

00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:49.670
So for real time
applications, this is huge.

00:41:49.670 --> 00:41:51.760
So our front end
was an Angular app

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:54.250
that we wrote that
subscribed to this callback

00:41:54.250 --> 00:41:56.830
from Firestore that pulls
it in and draws our heart

00:41:56.830 --> 00:41:57.700
rates on a graph.

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:00.801
So end-to-end, it was device
up to IoT Core, which gets

00:42:00.801 --> 00:42:02.050
bridged int the Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:04.150
Pub/Sub had a Cloud
Function that pulls from it,

00:42:04.150 --> 00:42:05.470
shoves it into Cloud Firestore.

00:42:05.470 --> 00:42:09.264
Firestore triggered with its
callback into an Angular app.

00:42:09.264 --> 00:42:10.180
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:42:10.180 --> 00:42:10.540
GABE WEISS: Yeah.

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:10.930
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

